From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 13:52:49 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] hwcomposer: do not render while the display is off

With the display off, requestUpdate() handed every window update to Qt's
own timer, so the scene graph went on rendering at the refresh rate, and
swapToWindow() then dropped each frame without eglSwapBuffers, logging
"Swap requested while display is off" for every one of them.

That is wasted CPU, and on drivers that submit a frame's commands only on
eglSwapBuffers it is a leak: the Adreno 220 driver of the HP TouchPad kept
every dropped frame's 64 KB command buffers. After an hour or two with the
display off surface-manager held about 2800 GPU allocations, 216 MB, and
had no GPU address space left ("kgsl_mmu_get_gpuaddr: gen_pool_alloc
failed"); the first frame after the display came back on crashed its
render thread with a SIGSEGV, and the screen stayed dark until the
compositor had restarted.

While the display is off, keep the update as pending instead: sleepDisplay
(false) already delivers pending updates once the display is on again. A
frame that is rendered anyway is flushed, so its commands still reach the
GPU, and the warning is logged once.

On the TouchPad, a minute with the display off now leaves the compositor's
GPU allocations and CPU time where they were, and the first frame after
power-on reaches the panel 0.17 s after it is powered.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/hwcomposer_backend_v11.cpp
+++ b/hwcomposer_backend_v11.cpp
@@ -517,9 +517,13 @@
 
 bool HwComposerBackend_v11::requestUpdate(QEglFSWindow *window)
 {
-    // If the display is off, do updates via the normal Qt-based timer.
-    if (m_displayOff)
-        return false;
+    // While the display is off, keep the window's update for when it comes back on (sleepDisplay(false)
+    // delivers it) instead of handing it to Qt's timer, which went on rendering frames at the refresh
+    // rate that nobody could see.
+    if (m_displayOff) {
+        m_pendingUpdate.insert(window->window());
+        return true;
+    }
 
     if (m_vsyncTimeout.isActive()) {
         m_vsyncTimeout.stop();
--- a/hwcomposer_backend_v20.cpp
+++ b/hwcomposer_backend_v20.cpp
@@ -467,9 +467,13 @@
 
 bool HwComposerBackend_v20::requestUpdate(QEglFSWindow *window)
 {
-    // If the display is off, do updates via the normal Qt-based timer.
-    if (m_displayOff)
-        return false;
+    // While the display is off, keep the window's update for when it comes back on (sleepDisplay(false)
+    // delivers it) instead of handing it to Qt's timer, which went on rendering frames at the refresh
+    // rate that nobody could see.
+    if (m_displayOff) {
+        m_pendingUpdate.insert(window->window());
+        return true;
+    }
 
     if (m_vsyncTimeout.isActive()) {
         m_vsyncTimeout.stop();
--- a/hwcomposer_context.cpp
+++ b/hwcomposer_context.cpp
@@ -51,6 +51,7 @@
 #include "hwcomposer_backend.h"
 
 #include <qcoreapplication.h>
+#include <GLES2/gl2.h>
 
 QT_BEGIN_NAMESPACE
 
@@ -138,7 +139,16 @@
 void HwComposerContext::swapToWindow(QEglFSContext *context, QPlatformSurface *surface)
 {
     if (display_off) {
-        qWarning("Swap requested while display is off");
+        // A frame rendered anyway is not presented, but its commands must still go to the GPU: drivers
+        // that submit them only on eglSwapBuffers (Adreno 2xx) otherwise keep every such frame's
+        // command buffers, until the process runs out of GPU address space (the HP TouchPad's
+        // surface-manager died with a SIGSEGV on waking up after an hour or two with the display off).
+        static bool warned = false;
+        if (!warned) {
+            qWarning("Swap requested while display is off");
+            warned = true;
+        }
+        glFlush();
         return;
     }
 
